rounds: "Eight review rounds. Every dimension that judges code came back clean in every round - the rename touched no behaviour, and no round found one. All nine verified findings were false sentences in this WU's own report or notes: an invented grep total, several counts that did not match their own lists, a check reported as passed in a form no fixture rename can satisfy ('all eight assertions unedited' - the rename reaches inside seven of the eight tests, since each query names its option), a claim about what the plan's surfaces table contained, a phrase attributed to a document that does not contain it, and - oldest of all, standing since the first result commit - the claim that this tip carried its own evidence notes while they sat on 7d7eba7, five correction commits behind - and, in round 7, that same 'unedited' clause riding along inside this note after round 6 rewrote only its rounds: field. No test changed after round 1: the test evidence below is what landed in 89b6bb7 and it has been re-derived from the tree in rounds 3 through 7, each of which re-ran every suite green independently. Round 8 verified none of the two it raised: it read this note in its corrected state and reported round 7's finding as unsupported, then flagged the doc/v1 note for repeating it. Refuting that produced the one lesson worth carrying out of this WU's review - a git note is a MUTABLE surface, so a claim about what a note once said is uncheckable unless a refs/notes commit sits beside it. The state round 7 read is at refs/notes/test 0f47698; the overwrite is ba48c69. The result report's round-7 row now cites that hash. Round 9 verified one more claim about the review itself - the section had kept running tallies and the last of them counted two round-8 candidates over a table listing one - so the tallies are gone and the per-candidate rows, ordered round 1 to 8, are the count."
